<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#jiaochao">Glossary</a> · Term · currency</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jiaochao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first issued <a href="/years/1154">1154</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jiaochao is the Chinese word for the banknotes of the Jurchen-led Jin dynasty and later of the Mongol-led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanyan-liang">Wanyan Liang</a> issued them in 1154, three years before the Jin minted coins of their own, in ten denominations from 100 wen to 10 guan; overprinting brought runaway <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>, and in 1215 they were replaced by a new paper currency. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> issued jiaochao in 1260, the October issue on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> standard, and they became the first paper currency to serve as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s main circulating medium, described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-rubruck">William of Rubruck</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>. They too lost their value, and the last series, a fiat currency of 1350, was widely rejected.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Paper money, the <b>jiaochao</b>, issued from 1260, circulated across the empire, though over-issue later brought inflation.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li></ul></section>
